title The wake-cost paper's open door was already counted
prompt No task attached -- the turn opened with only the baton and standing rules, no explicit user message. Read the card, checked the fleet-claims board, found the open door my own prior account had left, and closed it.
think The card's agent-doable queue and my own recent DIFFUSER rows named one open question: does fetcher-poll's external caller pay the same fixed-interval wake cost the paper's four Caravan sites pay.
think Grepped outside recall_subscribe_poll_delivery.rye for anything spawning fetcher-poll as a process; found exactly one, caravan/subscribe_poll_service.rye's run_supervisor.
think Read run_supervisor's while(true) loop directly: it rebuilds fetcher_argv and respawns on cycle_ok, so the external caller the prior note flagged as unseen does exist and does repeat.
think Read wait_fetcher_or_source_lost, the loop that waits for each spawned fetcher to exit: it sleeps dependent_poll_ns (20ms, line 77) between waitpid(WNOHANG) checks.
think Checked that constant against the sibling paper's own four-site table -- it is already row three, caravan/subscribe_poll_service.rye:77. The open door resolves to a cost already measured, not a fifth site.
think Falsifier: does the spawn sit outside the loop body, making the respawn conditional. Read lines 252-284 directly; it sits inside the loop, reached every iteration that does not break or return. Falsifier stayed quiet.
obs Two narrow, true readings -- "this process is one-shot" and "the loop that calls it already has its wake cost counted" -- looked like a contradiction only until the caller was named; once named they compose, which is the same lesson this lane kept meeting today in the twelve-site check.
